  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened capacities of the tripartite partners to engage and influence labour-related policies</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Building upon its ongoing efforts to tackle unacceptable forms of work (UFW) and promote gender equality in the labour market, the ILO will continue to support constituents to influence employment policies with the aim of reinforcing protections against violence and harassment (V&amp;H), enhancing pay equity, and advancing care economy initiatives within the OPT. 

As part of its commitment to creating safer workplaces, the ILO supported the General Federation of Trade Unions (PGFTU) in developing its first strategy for Gender Equality for 2024-2028. The strategy places emphasis on key areas dedicated to enhancing working conditions, generating new economic opportunities for women through skills development, and investing in workplace childcare facilities. The strategy focuses among others, on strengthening the role of women trade unionists in sectoral unions as well as leadership and decision-making process.   

The ILO is dedicated to supporting legal and policy reforms, including labour law provisions aimed at the elimination of all forms of discrimination. This includes backing the operationalization of the national pay equity committee and enhancing the capacities of its members to ensure equitable pay across genders. This work builds upon and expands on previous work of the ILO with the National Committee on Women&apos;s Employment (NCWE) in OPT, focusing on gender-based discrimination and promoting policy dialogue in alignment with ILO Conventions C-100 and C-111. 

